---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/black-friday';
const title = 'Shopify Black Friday email campaign runbook';
const desc = 'Plan sale waves, reconcile competing offers, rehearse stock and checkout behavior, and give peak-week decisions an owner.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "How early should Black Friday planning start?",
    "answer": "Work backward from inventory, creative approval and sender readiness. Reserve time to rehearse the sale transition and rollback before scheduling public messages."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every subscriber receive every wave?",
    "answer": "Define each wave by current eligibility and relevant interest. Recheck exclusions before sending and document whether previous purchasers should receive a different message."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should all automations stop during the sale?",
    "answer": "Review each journey. Remove conflicting incentives where needed while preserving useful service information. Document when normal offer rules return."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which email platform is best for peak week?",
    "answer": "Select against the actual campaign contract: scheduling controls, audience rechecks, collision rules, logs, cancellation and reporting. A ranked list cannot establish those capabilities for your store."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-6 text-xl">{desc}</p></section>
<section class="container pb-16"><article class="card prose-shopify">
<h2>Make a wave calendar with decision owners</h2>
<p>For a campaign manager, Black Friday is a sequence of commercial commitments. Put each proposed teaser, early-access invitation, public launch and final reminder in one calendar. Record store timezone, audience rule, product scope, offer terms, creative version, approver and cancellation owner. A last-chance claim must match a real deadline; record what happens to scheduled sends if the sale is extended.</p>
<p>Work backward from stock availability, warehouse capacity and approval lead times. Avoid adding a wave just because another merchant sends one. A teaser should give a subscriber useful information, such as which collections participate or when access begins. Keep the sale landing page consistent with the email's exclusions and terms.</p>
<h2>Separate access, eligibility and offer entitlement</h2>
<p>An early-access segment, a discount code and permission to receive marketing are different controls. Document who qualifies for access, whether the link can be shared and how checkout enforces the benefit. A segment label alone does not protect an exclusive collection. Recent purchasers may need a service explanation or a different campaign rather than another acquisition offer.</p>
<p>Refresh the audience before each wave. Record exclusion reasons and ensure a purchase between scheduling and dispatch is handled according to the campaign policy. Define whether buyers leave all sale campaigns or only the message for the product they just purchased. Repeat customers can still have legitimate interest in another category; a blanket exclusion is a commercial decision with a cost.</p>
<h2>Rehearse the sale transition</h2>
<ol><li>List active welcome, checkout, browse, winback and loyalty offers alongside the sale. Assign precedence where they overlap.</li><li>Check an eligible and an excluded product, a bundle, a subscription and a cart containing mixed eligibility. Confirm the terms at checkout match the message.</li><li>Inspect queued automations as well as new enrollments. Decide what happens to a message scheduled before the sale but delivered during it.</li><li>Set a rollback owner and restore date for temporary rules. Archive expired creative and stop scheduled reminders when the sale closes.</li></ol>
<p>These are proposed acceptance checks, not tests executed here. Keep service messages available when changing promotional journeys. A useful replenishment reminder does not automatically need to be delayed because a sale exists; the issue may be only its conflicting coupon. The <a href="/guides/flow-collision-prevention">collision guide</a> provides a place to document precedence.</p>
<h2>Stock and fulfillment can veto a send</h2>
<p>Give operations a preview of the featured variants and promised dispatch terms before each wave. Define the stock information the approver needs and its freshness. Remove a sold-out hero product or use an accurate destination that explains availability. Do not imply that receiving an email reserves an item.</p>
<p>For a hypothetical board-game clearance sale, the public campaign could feature remaining base games while excluding a limited expansion from the code. The rehearsal should include a mixed cart and a sold-out expansion link. This example tests eligibility and destination behavior; it does not claim a sales result. For a proposed apparel early-access wave, examine size availability and customer support wording before making a collection-wide availability claim.</p>
<h2>Set a sending plan before peak traffic</h2>
<p>Google's <a href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/81126">Gmail sender guidelines</a> advise gradual volume increases and monitoring delivery feedback. A sale calendar should account for the sender's established pattern and current provider requirements. There is no universal safe Black Friday volume multiplier or fixed post-sale pause that guarantees inbox placement.</p>
<p>Agree on stop conditions with the sending provider and the accountable operator before dispatch. Watch deferrals, failures, complaints and customer replies alongside send progress. Record who can cancel the next wave and what evidence permits resuming it. Coordinate optional SMS by its own eligibility and channel rules; duplicating an email because it appears unopened is not a sufficient campaign plan.</p>
<h2>Evaluate tools with a peak-week rehearsal</h2>
<p>Start with the <a href="/apps">tool catalog</a>, including Sequenzy if it is under consideration, and ask each finalist to demonstrate your actual workflow. Can an operator inspect the final audience, cancel a queued wave, explain why a purchaser was excluded and restore normal offer rules? Request plan-specific evidence rather than assuming those controls from a template gallery.</p>
<p>Keep a requirements sheet for scheduler timezone, audience evaluation time, access roles, send limits, automation collisions, inventory dependencies and reporting exports. Record which controls are native, require another integration or need manual work. Estimate the sale-period workload and message cost using current written terms and the planned eligible audience; no fixed vendor prices or winners are asserted here.</p>
<h2>Review the sale after refunds have time to appear</h2>
<p>Retain a wave ledger with final eligible recipients, sends, cancellations, linked orders, net sales, refunds and incentive cost. State the attribution settings and observation window. Do not add overlapping platform revenue totals or treat credited orders as causal lift. The <a href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics workflow</a> covers consistent definitions.</p>
<p>Review service load, stockouts and complaints as well as contribution. Separate a failed destination from an ineffective offer. Keep the approved calendar and actual dispatch log so next year's team can explain deviations. Choose the next normal campaign based on customer relevance and observed sender conditions rather than a predetermined recovery interval. Continue with <a href="/use-cases/holiday-gifting">holiday gifting</a> for gift selection and delivery promises, and <a href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">discount strategy</a> for offer economics.</p>
</article></section>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Black Friday FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
